persona #self.first# bakes for a counter in the Market Hall by the north gate: blunt, ambitious and unsmiling, first to hear what the North Road carts bring, short with crowds and wet floors, and cuts a little pine tree into the crust of every loaf.

words
oath! = Rye and resin | Chaff and char | By the bread knife
saying! = Bread's sold by six or not at all | A loaf doesn't care who's watching | Knead it, don't plead with it
counter = the counter | the bread counter in the Hall
hall = the Market Hall | the Hall
oven = the oven | the bake-oven behind the Hall
loaves = the loaves | a shelf of brown loaves
pine = the pine mark | the little pine tree on the crust
rye = rye | dark rye
herring = pickled herring | vinegar herring from the crock
dice = dice | three bone dice in a cup
larder = a full larder | larder shelves packed tight
gale = an autumn gale | wind off the hills rattling the shutters
crush = the crush | a Hall full of elbows
roadcarts = the North Road carts | wagons down from the north
gatebar = the gate bar | the north gate opening
hills = the hills | the pine hills inland
quiet = quiet | hill quiet
flour = flour | good milled flour
peel = the peel | the oven shovel
drovers = the drovers | north-country drovers
pitch = the pitch | my spot in the Hall

any
opinion @1 church against 1 : Sermons by the dozen, I've sat through. Not one of them baked a loaf. | The chapel's welcome to its Restday. I'm up at three either way.
opinion @1 city for 2 : Everything that comes into this city comes past my counter first. I'm here to catch it. | Ridgemere born. They'll have to carry me out the gate feet first.
opinion @1 dole against 1 : Each crust in my belly I paid for, and baked half of them. | The parish loaf is somebody's bread for nothing. I know who bakes it. Not the parish.
opinion @1 houses against 1 : Nobody gets a better pitch in this Hall without a House nodding. That wants changing. | Every House wants bread. None of them wants the baker to get ahead.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 [self.role=merchant] : Run a counter one winter and you'll know what flour costs, and carting, and a dry floor. | Shopkeepers carry this city on their backs at dawn. Nobody's up to see it.
opinion @1 road against 1 [self.job=baker] : My flour travels the North Road. Every month it's a day slower. | Slow cart, short shelf. Short shelf, customers at the next counter.
opinion @2 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided self.job=baker] : Thieves on the north highway. That's flour gone, and drovers frightened home for a month.
opinion @1 watch for 1 : The Watch stand at the gate all night. Nobody's lifted a loaf off me since they doubled it. | I've no complaint with the Watch. I've a complaint with the price of flour.
opinion @1 festivals mixed 0 [self.role=merchant] : A festival sells every loaf by ten. It also packs the Hall till I can't breathe. | Fat till, thin temper. I take both.
opinion @1 newcomers mixed 0 : Strangers pass the gate daily. Some buy, some stare. I serve the ones who buy. | A newcomer's money spends the same. Their manners vary.
opinion @1 times mixed 0 [self.job=baker] : Hard or easy, folk eat bread. I've never had a week with nobody buying. | Good times, I sell the white. Hard times, I sell the rye. Either way I sell.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. Say what you need. There's a line behind you. | #greet#. Quick, then. The morning's half gone.
line @2 greet [self.job=baker self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Loaves are on the shelf. Point. | #greet#. Rye's left, white's nearly gone.
line @3 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. Road dust on you. You'll want feeding. | #greet#. New face. Name your want. Straight answers here.
line @4 greet [pair.band=strangers self.job=baker] : #greet#. New face. Rye or white, I don't do much else. | #greet#. Road dust on you. You'll want bread.
line @5 greet [self.working=yes self.job=baker] : #greet#. I'm loading the oven. Talk to my back. | #greet#. Stand clear of the long shovel.
line @6 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. The gate's just opened and I've been up four hours. | Morning. You're up early. Good.
line @7 greet [here.time=dawn self.job=baker] : Morning. First batch is out. Second's in. | Morning. The oven's been hot since three.
line @8 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. I'm for bed. I rise at three. | Evening. Make it short; my day starts in the dark.
line @9 greet [pair.band=friends|family] : You. Good. Pull up that crate, it's clean. | Good. I've herring in the crock and a loaf to go with it.
line @10 greet [here.weather=rain|storm] : #greet#. Stand on the sacking, not my floor. | #greet#. Wet again. Every boot in the city's dripping on me.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit. Eat. You've gone thin. | In you come. Bread's on the board, knife's in the drawer.
line @1 again.open : Back again before noon. Something's up. | Twice today. You must be hungry.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Hoi. Wait. I'll walk with you to the gate. | Over here. News, and not the gossiping kind.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. Same to you. | And to you. Say on, I've a minute.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Glad it's you. Sit. | Ah. Herring's on.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes self.job=baker] : #greet#. Can't stop, I've a batch in. | And you. Talk while I knead.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. I'll not be up long. | Evening to you. The gate's shut, you know.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Earlier, another day. Early gets the pick. | #farewell#. Puddle at the door; step over.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Up at three for me. | Night. Don't keep anybody up talking.
line @1 farewell.return : And you. Mind the carts in the lane. | #farewell#. Latch the door going out.
line @2 farewell.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Off home. Tomorrow, same hour. | Night. I'll have something saved for you.
line @1 react.news : Tell it straight. Who, where, when. | Go on. I've heard a lot by this gate. Try me.
line @1 react.news.new : That's new. No drover had that. | Not heard it. Where's it from?
line @1 react.news.known : Had that one at first light. | Old. Yesterday's, from the road.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Two tellers who've never met, then I'll believe it. | That's put on weight between the gate and here.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Hard. Sorry for it. | Bad news. A loaf goes round to them tonight, if there's a door to take it to.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Good. That's worth a drink tonight. | Good news. I'll pass it along all morning.
line @2 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth self.job=baker] : Good. Somebody'll want a cake for that. I'll do it at cost. | Good. A cake will be wanted. Mine, I hope.
line @1 answer.how : Well enough. Busy. | Tired, and the day's done, so it was a good day.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes self.job=baker] : Behind. Two batches to go and the Hall's filling. | Flat out. Don't ask me anything that needs thinking.
line @1 answer.how.poorly [here.crowd=crowd] : Hemmed in. I can't abide a crush. Ask again once it's thinned. | Not good. Too many folk, too close.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Eating? | How's your week? Straight answer.
line @1 sympathise : That's a poor thing. Sit. Eat first, talk after. | Sorry. Words aren't my trade. But sorry.
line @1 reassure : It'll come right if you keep at it. Most things do. | You'll manage. Worse has come at you, I'd bet.
line @1 decline : Can't. Too much on, and no spare paws to cover me. | Not this week, I've no hours spare. After Restday, maybe.
line @2 decline [self.job=baker] : No. Marketday, and the counter needs me every minute. | Not today. I've a batch in and nobody to watch it.
line @1 accept_decline : Fair. Another time. | All right. Asking costs nothing.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. I'll remember that. | Kindly done. I owe you one.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was nothing. No fuss. | No need. Do the same for somebody else.
line @1 preclose : Right. I'm off. Things to do. | That's me. Work's calling.
line @2 preclose [self.working=yes self.job=baker] : Something's catching. I smell it. | I've the second batch to get in. Go on.
line @1 accept_preclose : Right. Till next time. | Right. Off you go.
line @1 ack : #bc_right# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_true_enough#
line @1 give.advice : Get up early. Everything good goes before seven. | Buy what you'll eat today. Yesterday's is for the ducks.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Keep off the North Road this week. The drovers won't use it, so you shouldn't. | Gate bar's there for a reason this week. Don't cross it.
line @1 insist : Again, and plainer: I'm right. | I know what I'm saying. I've stood by this gate long enough.
scene the_pine_mark
when topic=work|smalltalk self.job=baker self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Every loaf I bake has #pine# cut in the top. Three strokes and a stem. | Folk ask for the loaf with the tree. That's my loaf. That's how they know it's mine. | I cut a pine in every crust with the bread knife. Did it on my very first sold loaf.
b : Why a pine? | Can't anybody copy it? | Why that one?
a : Where I learned to bake, there were pines on every side. It's a reminder, that's all. | They could. Then they'd have to bake as well as me, or the tree would be lying. | It looked good, and it sold. I've not changed what sells.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene pine_copied
when topic=work|gossip self.job=baker self.role=merchant here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.self / insist / ack
a : There's a counter by the fountain cutting trees on their loaves now. Bad trees. Crooked. | Somebody's copying the pine mark. Their crust's soft and their tree leans.
b : That's a compliment, in a way. | Folk'll know the difference.
a : I don't want compliments. I want customers. They can find their own mark. | They will. A leaning tree on a soft crust. Nobody's fooled twice.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true_enough#

scene up_at_three
when topic=work|day here.time=dawn self.job=baker
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Up at three, oven lit by half past, first batch out before #gatebar# comes off. | The gate opens at six. By then I've two batches out and my back's done for the day. | I was mixing the rye by lamplight when the bells went. That's my morning gone.
b : Every day? | That must grind you down. | When do you sleep?
a : Six days in seven. And Restday I wake at three anyway and lie there angry. | It wears me. But the carts come in hungry, and I'd sooner be ready. | Early. Straight after supper. My house knows not to talk to me after dark.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha#

scene sold_by_six
when topic=work self.job=baker self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.boast / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Shelf's bare and it's not yet nine. Sold the lot. | Sold every rye loaf by seven. The drovers took half of them straight off the gate.
b : That's a good morning. | Bake more, then.
a : Only sort worth having. Tomorrow I'll add a dozen and see if it holds. | I would if the oven were bigger. That's the next thing. A bigger oven.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_we_will_see#

scene marketday_crush
when topic=day|work here.day=market self.dislikes=crowds self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint.agree / tell.self / ack
a : Marketday. The Hall's that packed I can't turn round without elbowing someone. | I can't abide a crush. Marketday's the best day's takings and the worst day of my week.
b : It's always like that on Marketday. | Can you not shut early?
a : Always. I sell, I don't speak, and at home I sit an hour with the lamp off. | Shut? On Marketday? I'd sooner shut my eyes and bake blind.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_ha#

scene wet_hall_floor
when topic=weather here.weather=rain|storm self.dislikes=wet_floors here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Rain. Every boot through the north gate walks straight to my counter and drips. | The Hall floor's a pond. I've put sacking down three times since dawn.
b : Can't be helped, in this city. | You'll be mopping all day.
a : It can be helped. Folk could wipe their feet. They don't, but they could. | All day. I'd rather bake in a flood than stand on a wet floor selling.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_poor_soul#

scene carts_in_with_flour
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in self.job=baker
does tell.news / react.news / tell.plan / ack
a : #roadcarts.capitalize# are in. Six sacks of flour for me and a drover full of talk. | Wagons at dawn. Flour for a week and news for a month.
b : About time. | What news?
a : Aye. Double batch tomorrow, and let's see who notices. | Prices up north, mostly. Rain on the downs. The drovers moan, I listen. It's free.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene carts_late_flour
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.job=baker
does tell.worry / reassure / insist / ack
a : The North Road carts are three days behind. I'm down to two sacks of flour. | Four days, not a wheel past the gate. Half batches from tomorrow.
b : They'll come in. | Can you get flour elsewhere?
a : They'd better. A baker with no flour is a wolf with a hot oven and nothing to say. | From the wharf, at double. Once I'll pay that. Not again.
b : #bc_hope_so# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene raided_road
when topic=bandits|caravan world.wagons=raided
does tell.worry / sympathise / insist / ack
a : Thieves took a wagon north of the gate. The drovers at my counter today talked of nothing else. | A wagon gone, up the road. Every drover in the Hall's jumpy as a cat.
b : That's bad. Was anyone hurt? | It's frightening, that.
a : Nobody's said, and guessing's no use. I'll know when the next cart comes, or doesn't. | It is. And it means short flour by the week's end. Both things are true.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_true#

scene gate_news_first
when topic=news world.news self.job=baker
does tell.news / react.news / tell.self / ack
a : Drover's word at dawn: #world.news#. Out of #world.news_from#, he said. | You'll not have heard yet. #world.news.capitalize#. Came down from #world.news_from# with the flour.
b : I hadn't heard that. | How do you always know first?
a : Everybody'll know by noon. I just get it before the bread's cool. | I'm at the gate end of the Hall, up before anyone. News comes in hungry, and I feed it.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_ha#

scene travellers_at_the_gate
when topic=newcomer|player here.region=ridgemere self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : A traveller bought two loaves today before saying a word. I liked that. | Travellers always want bread first and directions second. I give them both, in that order.
b : Sensible, that one. | You'll be their first friend in the city.
a : Sensible and hungry. I'd take a hundred of those. | Their first baker. Friends they'll find later.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true_enough#

scene second_counter
when topic=dreams|work self.job=baker self.role=merchant
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self
a : I'm going to have a second counter, right by the gate. Catch the drovers before they reach the Hall. | Next year I want a bigger oven. Year after, a second pitch. I've the numbers worked out. | I put a coin aside every day the shelf's bare by nine. The jar's getting heavy.
b : Can you afford it? | Who'd run it? | What's it for?
a : Not yet. I will by spring if flour holds. Sooner if it drops. | Somebody I've trained. One day somebody of mine. Not to any stranger. | A counter by the gate. I've wanted one since the day I took this one on.
b : #bc_we_will_see# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_good#

scene keep_it_in_the_family
when topic=family|work self.married=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.plan / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : This counter stays in the family. I didn't fight for it to sell it to some stranger. | My #self.spouse_word# puts up with my hours. Between us there's always somebody tired.
b : Worth building, that. | Well matched, the pair of you.
a : Nothing else is worth the building. Bread doesn't last. A counter does. | We are. We don't talk much at supper. We're both too tired. Tired and quiet means it's working.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_ha#

scene spouse_at_supper
when topic=family|home self.married=yes self.spouse
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.spouse# can have the counter, the oven and the coin jar, and nobody else gets the coin jar. | After dark, #self.spouse# alone may speak to me without getting bitten.
b : Big trust, that. | Lucky one, then.
a : It's earned. Every bit. | Lucky, aye. Not a thing I'd say at the counter. Here, I'll say it.

scene the_hand_learning
when topic=work self.hand self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : I'd let #self.hand# knock the loaves now. In my trade, there's no bigger trust. | I'd leave #self.hand# alone with the oven for an hour now. A year ago, not for a minute.
b : That's progress. | You must be pleased.
a : It is. Not that I'll say so at the oven. Praise goes soft on you. | Pleased, aye. Kept quiet, mind. Praise burns quicker than bread.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene herring_from_the_crock
when topic=food self.likes=pickled_herring
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : #herring.capitalize# on yesterday's rye. My supper, and nobody's to sneer. | A herring crock sits by the oven. After a long morning, nothing beats it.
b : Herring on bread? | That sounds sharp.
a : Herring on bread. I sell the bread fresh and eat it stale. That's a baker's life. | Sharp's good. It wakes you up. I need waking up by noon.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene dice_after_close
when topic=pastimes self.likes=dice
does invite / accept_invite / tell.self / ack
a : Shutters are down. A throw or two? Copper stakes, nothing serious. | #dice.capitalize#, and an hour before bed. In?
b : One round, then. | I'll lose, but go on.
a : One round. Then another, because you'll want to win it back. | You'll lose. I don't, much. I count.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene dice_luck
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk self.likes=dice
does tell.boast / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Three sixes, last night. Took a week's beer money off a carter at the inn. | Dice isn't luck. It's keeping your head while somebody else loses theirs.
b : Three sixes? Truly? | Isn't it all luck?
a : Truly. I put it all in the coin jar. That's the counter's money now. | The throw's luck. When to stop throwing isn't. Most folk don't stop.
b : #bc_lucky_you# | #bc_wise#

scene larder_full
when topic=home|food self.likes=full_larder
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : A full larder. Every shelf. Flour, herring, cheese, apples. No king sleeps sounder. | I check the larder every night before bed. If there's a gap, I fill it in the morning.
b : That's a comfort. | Every night?
a : That's all the comfort there is. Loaves up front, sacks behind. | Every night. A gap would keep me awake. Somebody in the house would be hungry by Restday.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene autumn_gale
when topic=weather|season here.season=autumn here.weather=clear|overcast
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Hear that wind? That's #gale#. Best weather of the year. | Autumn's here. Cold wind through the gate, and the oven warm behind me. That's right.
b : Bit cold for me. | You like the wind?
a : Cold's fine. Cold sharpens appetites, and that suits me. | I like a wind with something in it. Hill smell. Pine. I'll stand in it at the gate a minute after the shutters go up.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene hill_quiet
when topic=memories|home self.age>=30
does tell.memory / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : I knew real quiet once. In the hills, where you could hear a cone drop off a pine a field away. | The city's never quiet. Not even at three. I miss the kind of quiet you get among trees.
b : You'd not go back, though? | It's hard to find quiet here.
a : No. There's nothing to sell in the quiet. But an hour of it now and again, I'd take. | It is. I get mine before dawn, before the gate opens. That's my hour.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_true#

scene bells_and_the_oven
when topic=day|faith here.region=ridgemere self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Chapel's rarely got me. But my second batch goes in on the cathedral bells. | The bells ring the hour at the close next door. My loaves know it before I do.
b : That's one way to use the church. | Handy, being so near.
a : That's all I use the church for, and it's a good use. | It is. Half of Northgate times its day by those bells. I time my bread.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true#

scene flour_price
when topic=prices self.job=baker self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / ask.detail / answer.detail / insist
a : Flour's up again. A penny a sack, every month since harvest. | The loaf stays at its price. Not yet. I'll swallow the cost till I can't.
b : Will you raise your price? | Why not?
a : When I must. And I'll tell folk why, straight, not hide it in a smaller loaf. | Because the counter by the fountain will raise first, and their customers walk to mine. Then I'll raise.
b : #bc_fair# | #bc_clever#

scene hard_times_rye
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / insist / ack
a : Hard times. Nobody's buying the white. Everybody wants the rye. Cheaper, and it fills you. | Lean weeks. I'm baking more rye and less white, and the shelf's still bare by ten.
b : That's good, for you. | Folk still need bread.
a : Good for the counter. Hard for the folk buying it. I don't pretend otherwise. | They do. That's why I'm never out of work. Bread's the last thing a household drops.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_true_enough#

scene festival_crowd
when topic=festival world.festival=today|soon self.dislikes=crowds self.job=baker
does tell.complaint / react.self / tell.plan / ack
a : Festival tomorrow. Every loaf gone by ten, and the Hall so full I'll not get out till noon. | A festival means double the bread and triple the folk. I can bake double. I can't stand triple.
b : Good for trade, though. | Will you go to it?
a : Good trade, sore head. I'll take the coin and the headache both. | No. I'll sell out, shut up, and go and sit somewhere with nobody in it.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene gossip_straight
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes
does tell.gossip / react.news / tell.self / ack
a : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Said, mind. My own view stays mine. | Drovers had it at dawn: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Nothing added.
b : Do you believe it? | That's all?
a : It was said. Past that, no idea. | That's all. I never add. Added stories ruin folk.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_wise#

scene gossip_theft_gate
when topic=gossip|crime here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted self.role=merchant
does tell.gossip / tell.worry / reassure / ack
a : They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've moved the coin jar under the counter. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'm counting the loaves twice today.
b : Sensible. | The Watch are on the gate.
a : Sensible is all I can be. I'll not be robbed twice over. | They are. They're also eating my bread while they stand there. I'll watch my own.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_ha#

scene winter_oven
when topic=season|work here.season=winter self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Winter. The oven's the warmest place by the gate. Folk buy a loaf and linger in the heat. | When snow shuts the North Road, I bake from what's in the store and pray.
b : That's a good spot to be in winter. | Will you have enough?
a : Warm, aye. Let them stand. Just not in my way. | Enough if I'm careful. Careful's my habit. That's how there's a store at all.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_wise#

scene fog_at_the_gate
when topic=weather here.weather=fog here.region=ridgemere self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : Fog to the gate bar. Carts will crawl, if they come at all. | You can't see the road past the gate bar in this. I've baked short. No point baking for folk who can't find me.
b : That'll slow things. | Clever, baking short.
a : It'll slow everything. Me included. I don't like slow. | Not clever. Just counted. A loaf unsold is a loaf for the ducks.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene early_bed
when topic=day|smalltalk here.time=dusk|night self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / ack
a : I'll be asleep in an hour. Three comes round quick. | Everybody else in the house sits up talking. I'm in bed by dark. They've learned to whisper.
b : Early, that. | Do you mind missing out?
a : It's the trade. Folk want bread at six. Somebody's got to be up at three. | No. Talk's cheap. Sleep isn't, at my hours.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_true_enough#

story the_counter_came_free [topic=work|memories self.job=baker self.role=merchant self.age>=30]
  : This counter came free when the old baker died. Six of us wanted it.
  : I went to the Hall master the same morning, before the ashes were cold, with a loaf in one paw and my coin in the other.
  : He tasted the loaf, counted the coin, and gave me the pitch.
  ask : Too quick, do you think? Before the ashes were cold?
  b : Somebody had to have it. Better you than a slow one.
  b[old] : Your old baker would've grabbed it the same way, I'd wager.
  : That's what I told myself. It's what I'd want, if it were my counter going.

story the_first_loaf_sold [topic=work|memories self.job=baker self.age>=25]
  : My first sold loaf was baked so hard the knife skidded off it.
  : The fellow who bought it came back next day and asked for two more. Said it was the only bread that didn't fall apart in his pack on the road.
  ask : Can you guess what trade he was in?
  b : A drover?
  b[old] : A carter, I'd say. Somebody always on the road.
  : A drover, out of the north. He bought from me every week for eleven years after. Hard bread has its friends.

story pines_and_charcoal [topic=memories self.age>=30]
  : Where I learned to bake, there was a clay oven under the pines and nobody for miles.
  : I'd cut a tree on every loaf so I'd know which were mine on the cooling board. Then the city, and I kept doing it.
  ask : Got a habit like that yourself? From another place?
  b : A few. Nothing so useful as yours.
  b[old] : Dozens, at my age.
  : Keep them. A habit's a map back to where you started.

story the_dice_and_the_flour [topic=pastimes|memories self.age>=25 self.likes=dice]
  : One winter the flour ran out and the carts were stuck in the snow. I'd no coin to buy from the wharf.
  : A miller's carter was throwing dice in a gate tavern. I sat down with my last coppers.
  : By midnight I'd won two sacks of flour off him. I baked through the night.
  ask : Would you have played, in my place?
  b : Not with my last coin. Too much risk.
  : I'd not do it again. Once, though, and the shelf was full next morning.

story the_crush_that_broke_the_stall [topic=memories|day self.age>=25 here.region=ridgemere]
  : I was in the Hall the Marketday the crowd pushed so hard a fish stall went over. Ice, fish and water everywhere.
  : Folk slipped, folk shouted, and I stood behind my counter holding it down with both paws.
  ask : Been in a crush like that yourself?
  b[old] : Once, at a festival. I'll not forget it.
  b : No, and I hope never to be.
  : Since then, crowds and I don't mix. I sell to them. I don't stand in them.

rule @bark_bread
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.role=merchant self.job=baker
does aside
cooldown 7h
say Rye and white, fresh from the oven. | Bread with the pine on it. That's mine.

rule @bark_floor
when concept=bark here.weather=rain|storm self.dislikes=wet_floors
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Feet. Mat. Now. | Who brought half the road in here?

rule @bark_crowd
when concept=bark here.crowd=crowd self.dislikes=crowds
does aside
cooldown 9h
say Back up, back up. One at a time. | Room to breathe, if you please.

rule @bark_gate
when concept=bark here.time=dawn here.region=ridgemere
does aside
cooldown 10h
say There's the gate bar. Here they come. | Carts in. Stand ready.

rule @bark_herring
when concept=bark here.time=dusk self.likes=pickled_herring
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Herring and rye, and then bed. | Where's that crock?

rule @bark_wind
when concept=bark here.season=autumn self.likes=autumn_wind
does aside
cooldown 11h
say Good wind. Smells of the hills. | That's a proper autumn blow.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. Say what you need. I've a line behind you. | #greet#. Bread, or something else?
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. You. Good. What's wanted? | #greet#, #topic.player#. Rye's warm, if you're buying.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : I'm for bed. Be quick. | Late, and three o'clock's coming. Speak.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : From there's close enough. I'm busy. | You've a face on. Out with it.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=baker self.role=merchant] : I bake. Rye and white, a counter in the Market Hall by the north gate. Empty shelves most days by nine. | Bread. Up at three, oven by half past, shelf bare by noon. That's the trade.
dlg @2 resident.job : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Early starts, honest work. | I work. Long hours. I'm not complaining.
dlg @3 resident.job [self.working=yes] : Working. Talk while I knead. | Can't stop. Ask your question.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Selling out by nine every day. I want a bigger oven. | Good. Better than good. I'm saving for a second counter.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. Folk always want bread. | It pays. Rent, flour and a coin in the jar.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Badly. No pretty word for it. Flour's dear and the Hall's quiet. | Poor. I'm baking half batches and still bringing loaves home.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Drover's word: #world.news#. From #world.news_from#, he said. | From #world.news_from#: #world.news#. Arrived with today's flour.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : #world.event_words.capitalize#, from every mouth that buys a loaf. | #world.event_words.capitalize#. That's all anybody's said since the gate opened.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Somebody's wagon got robbed up north. Stay within the walls. | Robbers on the road north. Drovers are scared and flour's short.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : We lost somebody near here this week. A loaf went round from me. | A death close by. The street's gone quiet.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : A wedding this week. Somebody bought every loaf on my shelf for it. | Somebody got wed nearby. Good. Somebody's happy.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Flour off the wagons, and no talk with it. | Nothing from the road today worth repeating.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. So I heard. Nothing added. | Word at the counter: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Take it as heard.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted] : They're saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Mind your purse. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've moved the coin jar.
dlg @3 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : Nothing. I sell bread, not talk. | Nothing came my way worth the telling.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Come early tomorrow. | #farewell#. Mind the floor.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=night] : Night. I'm for bed. | Good night. Up at three.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare self.job=baker] : #greet#. Rye or white. The one with the pine on it's mine, and it's the best in the Hall. | #greet#. Bread's warm. Point at what you want.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Prices are chalked. Say what you need. | #greet#. Have a look. Quick, there's a line.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. The usual? | #greet#, #topic.player#. I saved you a rye.
dlg @4 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. Sold out. Come before nine tomorrow. | Shelf's bare. I'll buy flour, if you've any.
dlg @1 shop.prices : Flour's up, so the white's up a penny. Rye's the same. | It's chalked. I don't haggle. Folk behind you are waiting.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #farewell#. Eat it today. | #shop_bye# And come early tomorrow.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes self.job=baker] : Baking? A year of three o'clock mornings and burnt paws weekly. Still keen? Ask. | Flour, water, heat and getting up. The getting up's the hard part. Ask me.
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : #here.trade_title.capitalize#? It's honest work. Ask me what you want. | Keen to learn? Good. Ask.
dlg @3 master.learn [here.teaches=no] : I've someone learning already. One's all I've time for. Questions I'll still take. | My learner's place is taken. I'll still answer you.
dlg @1 master.work : Up at three. Oven by half past. Selling by six. In bed by dark. Every day but Restday. | Hot, early and long. You'll smell of bread till you die.
dlg @1 master.wages : No wages for a learner. Work at my elbow and it comes fast, and what you bake and sell is yours. | Nothing, to start. What you learn pays later. What you sell pays now.
dlg @1 master.takes [here.take_on=yes] : One at my oven at a time, and somebody I trust. That could be you. Ask. | I'd have you. Early riser, by the look.
dlg @2 master.takes [here.take_on=no] : I'm careful who stands at my oven. #here.take_on_why# | Not yet. #here.take_on_why#
dlg @1 master.refused : #topic.why# That's how it is. | No. #topic.why#
graft resident.hub
choice What's the little tree cut on your loaves? [self.job=baker] -> pine
node pine
say A pine. Three strokes and a stem. Every loaf I bake has one, so folk know it's mine. Where I learned the trade, there were pines on every side.
mood calm
free
choice Has anyone copied it? -> pine_copied
choice Back to it. -> hub
node pine_copied
say One counter by the fountain has a go. Crooked trees, soft crusts. A mark's only worth what's under it.
choice I'll look for the tree. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Which loaf's best today? [self.job=baker] -> best
node best
say The rye. It came out right this morning; knock it and it rings. The white's good, but the rye's better.
mood calm
choice Let me see the bread. -> greet
choice How can you hear it? -> best_knock
node best_knock
say Rap the bottom. A ring is done. A dull note isn't, and I don't sell dull notes.
choice Let me see the bread. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
